 (:adr/id "adr-2608319000-eleven-kotoba-slices-and-the-gate-that-runs-them")
 (:adr/title "Eleven Kotoba slices, and the gate that runs the compiled artifact")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
 (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608261100-migration-unit-is-the-vertical-slice" "adr-2608302100-single-file-kotoba-guests-cannot-reach-the-stdlib" "adr-2608650000-two-kinds-of-cannot"))
 (:adr/body "Eleven `.cljc` libraries got a Kotoba guest on 2026-08-31, and then all
eleven got a gate that runs the COMPILED artifact. Both halves are recorded
here because the second half is the one that was missing, and its absence
had exactly the shape this workspace keeps warning about.

## The slices

Each moved a decision -- never bytes, keys, clocks or sockets -- into a
`.kotoba` guest, held it against the `.cljc` oracle where one existed, and
broke every branch with its call sites left alone to see the named test go
red. What each one found:

| repo | the decision | what was measured |
|---|---|---|
| org-iso-opentype | SFNT table directory | 0xDEADBEEF version parses `:magic-ok? true`; an offset of 0xFFFFFFFF is handed to the caller; `numTables` 0x7FFF throws instead of refusing |
| org-chainagnostic-cacao | EIP-4361 validity window | the portable half has NO temporal check; `rfc3339-re` is a shape and not a calendar; nbf >= exp verifies inside the skew band |
| org-biscuitsec | delegated-scope intersection | a later block can INTRODUCE a kind; `:cap/holder` comes from the caller; expiry compared lexicographically |
| org-w3-webauthn | relying-party ceremony | \"Never throws\" throws; several strings name one challenge; crossOrigin unchecked; a counter that returns to zero passes |
| org-oasis-saml | SP acceptance | the decision has no home in the library; the emitter cannot produce an assertion a correct SP would accept |
| org-ietf-dnssec | canonical name order | the fold is locale-aware in a function whose docstring forbids it; a long label emits the compression marker; a dot is not always a separator |
| org-ietf-mime | message framing | the header block ends at the wrong blank line; transport padding hides a part; a field name may contain a space |
| org-ietf-x509 | validity + issuer authority | the caller's instant is never normalised; a signature verifies without the signer being a CA |
| org-ietf-tls | peer authentication | an expired certificate authenticates; a name a client WANTS may be a wildcard |
| org-pkware-zip | entry name | every Zip Slip name round-trips through this library's own writer and reader |
| org-oci-distribution | repository name and reference | `a/../b` and `MyApp` are their own repositories; a tag is anything without a colon |

## The gate, and why it was needed

Every one of those slices was verified by driving the guest through
`kotoba.kir`. Nothing had asserted that `kotoba -M compile` produces
something that answers the same way. A check that never ran looks exactly
like a check that passed.

All eleven now compile with the public CLI, instantiate on real
`WebAssembly` through amu's own `runtime/browser-host.mjs`, and have every
answer held against the interpreter's. They agree.

## What the gate cost, all measured

* THE BROWSER HOST REFUSES A MODULE WITH NO `main` (browser-host.mjs:371,
  and the runtime README's example is `exports.main()`). None of the eleven
  had one, so none could be hosted by the runtime they compile for. Each
  now has a `main` that is a self-check, not a stub.

* `main` IS BOUNDED FROM TWO SIDES. A module's fuel is a private global
  baked in at compile time (512 default) and is spent over the life of an
  INSTANCE, so `main`'s calls share one budget -- the TLS one had to shrink
  to two calls on three one-letter labels. The native backends evaluate
  `main` as a compile-time ORACLE with a budget of their own: ten calls is
  `native artifact oracle evaluation rejected`, three compiles.

* FUEL IS PER INSTANCE, NOT PER CALL. Reusing one instance answers
  `unreachable` a few calls in, which reads exactly like a wrong decision.
  Every probe call gets a fresh instance -- except a streaming walk, where
  the state must survive, which is why those tokens are small.

* NATIVE COMPILES, AND IS REFUSED BY `:document`. Six of the eleven compile
  to `aarch64-macos`; the five that do not are exactly the five that use
  `:document` values. It is the USE and not the export list -- making an
  export private leaves the refusal unchanged. The constraint is
  `:document`, NOT Wasm, and it is `:not-yet-implemented` rather than a
  security constraint (ADR-2608650000).

* NATIVE EXECUTION IS NOT MEASURED. `amu measure-runtime` builds the loader,
  but `amu sign` answers `native export table rejected` for this class of
  artifact -- on `aarch64-macos` and bare `aarch64`, with a full export
  table and with `main` alone. Compiled and admitted is measured; executed
  is not, and the tests say so rather than assuming it away.

* `offer-header` CANNOT BE CALLED FROM INSIDE ITS MODULE. An `internal
  compiler error` (exit 70, no detail) against amu 2cb7d3f. `offer-entry`
  in the same position compiles, and so does `init`; the byte vector's size
  is irrelevant. Calling it from the HOST is fine.
  `org-iso-opentype` holds both halves of that control as a ratchet.

## Two harness bugs, both of the class this workspace names

* The discrimination harness wrote its regexes inside a quoted heredoc, so
  `\\d` reached Python as a literal backslash and SIXTEEN runs that had all
  failed were reported as having no failures. A measuring instrument that
  answered \"clean\" for every run.

* The opentype probe took the header with `subvec`, which throws on the JVM
  and does not in ClojureScript, so the two halves of that gate disagreed
  about the HARNESS rather than about the runtimes.

## What discrimination changed in the guests

Four clauses were deleted because breaking them reddened nothing -- they
were covered by the rule below them and only looked like they decided:
webauthn's explicit `(= presented 0)`, tls's label-count comparison, oci's
empty-component clause, zip's `(= end 0)`. Chasing zip's turned up a real
gap (`a//` was accepted). Several tests exist only because a break was
invisible without them: an empty name, a non-ASCII name on each side
separately, a same-label-count mismatch, an unregistered algorithm with an
empty encoding, a missing NameID.

## Standing rules this leaves behind

* A new guest lands WITH its artifact gate, not with a follow-up.
* Keep the host boundary at string/i64/bool where the decision allows it;
  a `:document` boundary costs the native target today, and the header
  should say so.
* Skipping is not passing: measure a tool by RUNNING it and reading the
  exit code, never by `which`; a probe that cannot measure exits 3.
* Assert a refusal BY ITS REASON, so the test cannot stay green when the
  cause changes or the compiler breaks.

## Resume point

Next slice: `org-ietf-websocket` (handshake validation -- `Sec-WebSocket-Key`
and `accept-key`, `valid-key?`, `validate-server-response`; a string-only
boundary, so it should be native-capable). Read the repo before assuming
what is missing. Other unmigrated candidates surveyed but not started:
`org-ietf-rdap`, `org-ietf-epp`, `org-ietf-hpke`, `org-ietf-webdav`.

Not touched and not mine: a `codex/logic-authorizer-20260830` worktree on
`org-biscuitsec` at `6725eba`, and uncommitted changes in the superproject
under `90-docs/observatory/`, `scripts/fleet-ci/nodes.edn` and
`scripts/hermes-hyakka-bots/` belonging to other sessions.")))
